{{Politics of Okaiken}}
[[File:Diet_of_Okaiken.png|thumb|250px|The [[National Diet Building]] in [[Okami]]]]

In '''[[Okaiken]]''', politics are conducted in a framework of a dominant-party bicameral parliamentary representative democratic constitutional monarchy. A hereditary monarch, currently Emperor Masahito since 2011, serves as head of state while the Prime Minister of Okaiken, currently Honda Shigetsuma since November 2025, serves as the elected head of government.

Legislative power is vested in the [[National Diet of Okaiken]], which consists of the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)]] and the [[House of Officers]]. The House of Commoners has eighteen standing committees ranging in size from 20 to 50 members and The House of Officers has sixteen ranging from 10 to 45 members.

Executive power is vested in the Cabinet, which is led by the Prime Minister who is nominated by the National Diet and appointed by the Emperor. A coalition government led by the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) and the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) has held office since the 2025 general election.

Judicial power is vested in the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]] and lower courts, and sovereignty is vested in the people of Okaiken by the [[Constitution of Okaiken]]. Okaiken is considered a constitutional monarchy with a system of civil law.

The politics of Okaiken in the postwar period has largely been defined by the shifting dominance of major parties. While the Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) was historically the ruling force, the rise of the Progressive Party of Okaiken and the 2011 restorations have fundamentally altered the state structure.

==Constitution==
{{Main|Constitution of Okaiken}}
The Constitution of Okaiken ({{nihongo|岡井県国憲法|Okaiken-Koku Kenpō}}) serves as the fundamental law of the nation, having replaced the earlier Saito Constitution of 1870. Drafted in 1969 by the transitional government of [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] following the [[Quiet Revolution]], it was enacted on May 4, 1970. This document formally dissolved the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] and established the Federal Republic of Okaiken, introducing a parliamentary system and guaranteeing fundamental human rights. The text has seen significant updates in the 21st century, most notably the 2011 [[Jutsu Restoration]] which restored the Emperor as the Head of State ({{nihongo|元首|Genshu}}), and the 2014 Kurosawa Doctrine which amended Article 9 to allow for collective self-defense and the maintenance of a standing military.

The legal framework is organized into eleven chapters containing 103 articles. Chapter I defines the Emperor as a symbol of unity whose position is derived from the will of the people, while Chapter II addresses the renunciation of war and the protection of national assets. Fundamental human rights, such as equality under the law and freedom of thought, are protected in Chapter III. The remaining chapters establish the National Diet as the highest organ of state power, vest executive authority in the Cabinet, and ensure the independence of the judiciary. The constitution also outlines principles for local self-government and national finance, while Article 96 provides the specific mechanisms for amendments through a two-thirds majority in the Diet and a popular referendum.

==The Emperor and Imperial Household==
{{Main|Emperor of Okaiken}}
The [[Emperor of Okaiken]] is recognized as the symbol of the state and the unity of the people within the current constitutional monarchy. Serving primarily as a ceremonial figurehead, the monarch is also the head of the Sanese Imperial Family and the highest authority of the Sanese Animistic religion, traditionally viewed as a descendant of the goddess Amaterasu. The Emperor is referred to as the Tennō ({{nihongo|天皇|heavenly sovereign}}) or the Mikado. The Imperial House of Okaiken traces its lineage back to Emperor Toyama in 695 BC, making it the oldest continuing hereditary monarchy in the world. Historically, the role has shifted between absolute rule and ceremonial representation, particularly during the Honda Shogunate when military governors exercised political power while the Emperor held spiritual authority.

Under the 1970 constitution, the Emperor performs ceremonial acts of state only with the advice and approval of the Cabinet. These duties include the promulgation of laws and treaties, the convocation of the Diet, and receiving foreign dignitaries. The monarch formally appoints the Prime Minister as designated by the Diet and the Chief Judge of the Supreme Court as designated by the Cabinet. The Emperor does not possess powers related to government and does not serve as the commander-in-chief of the Self-Defense Forces, as that executive role is vested in the Prime Minister. The current monarch is His Imperial Majesty Emperor [[Masahito]], whose reign is known as the Jutsu era following his accession in 2011.

===Imperial Household Agency===
{{Main|Imperial Household Agency}}
The Imperial Household Agency ({{nihongo|宮内庁|Kunai-chō}}) is the government agency responsible for state matters concerning the Imperial House. It manages the daily lives, security, and health of the Imperial Family, as well as the preservation of the Privy Seal of Okaiken. Historically rooted in the ancient Imperial Household Ministry established by the Taihō Code, the Agency was restored in its modern form on 15 June 2011 following the Jutsu Restoration. It currently operates under the jurisdiction of the Cabinet Office and is led by the Grand Steward, a position currently held by Buichiro Kuroda.

The Agency is organized into several specialized boards, including the Board of Chamberlains, which manages the personal schedules of [[Emperor Masahito]] and [[Empress Michiko]], and the [[Crown Prince Tomohito|Crown Prince]]'s Household, which supports the scientific and industrial patronage of [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]. During the republican period (1968–2011), these functions were largely dormant or absorbed by the [[Ministry of Culture and Heritage]]. The modern Agency has faced debate regarding its focus on functional royalty and industrial efficiency, with some critics suggesting these priorities conflict with the throne's traditional spiritual role.

==The Prime Minister and Cabinet==
{{Main|Prime Minister of Okaiken|Cabinet of Okaiken}}
The Prime Minister of Okaiken ({{nihongo|内閣総理大臣|Naikaku-sōri-daijin}}) is the head of government of Okaiken. The office was created in 1885 and took its current form with the 1968 constitution. The Prime Minister is designated by both houses of the National Diet and must be a civilian and a member of the legislature. The current Prime Minister is Honda Shigetsuma, who took office on 25 August 2021.

As the head of the Cabinet, the Prime Minister exercises control and supervision over the entire executive branch. Key constitutional roles include the appointment and dismissal of Ministers of State, presenting bills to the Diet, and reporting on domestic and foreign relations. Under the Self-Defense Forces Act of 1969, the Prime Minister serves as the commander in chief of the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces. The official office of the Prime Minister is the Kantei ({{nihongo|官邸|Kantei}}), located in the heart of Okami.

The Cabinet ({{nihongo|内閣|Naikaku}}) is the executive organ collectively responsible to the Diet. It manages foreign affairs, concludes treaties, and administers the civil service. If the House of Commoners passes a motion of no confidence, the Cabinet must resign en masse unless the house is dissolved for a general election.

==Legislature==
{{Main|National Diet}}
The law-making institution, the National Diet ({{nihongo|国会|Kokkai}}), consists of two Houses: the House of Officers ({{nihongo|公議院|Kōgiin}}) and the House of Commoners ({{nihongo|衆議院|Shūgiin}}). Both houses are directly elected under the Direct Party and Representative Voting System (DPR), a form of proportional representation where seats are allocated based on party percentage in multi-member districts. The Diet is the highest organ of state power and is responsible for nominating the Prime Minister. It meets in the National Diet Building located in Okami.

The House of Commoners is the more powerful lower house, consisting of 475 members elected for four-year terms. It holds supremacy in matters of the budget, the ratification of treaties, and the designation of the Prime Minister. If the two houses disagree, the decision of the House of Commoners typically prevails after a mandatory waiting period. The lower house can be dissolved by the Prime Minister, a power frequently exercised to trigger snap elections. The 2025 general election resulted in the current 60th Diet, where the Progressive Party of Okaiken formed a coalition with the Okaiken Socialist Party.

The House of Officers is the 251-member upper house, acting as a body of continuity with fixed six-year terms. Half of its members are elected every three years, and the chamber cannot be dissolved by the Prime Minister. Known as the Seat of Reason ({{nihongo|良識の府|Ryōshiki no fu}}), it is designed to provide stability and oversight. While it can reject bills passed by the lower house, its veto can be overridden by a two-thirds majority in the House of Commoners.

==Judiciary==
{{Main|Judicial system of Okaiken}}
The judicial system of Okaiken is governed by the principles of independence and constitutional oversight. Article 76 of the Constitution guarantees that all judges are independent in the exercise of their conscience and are bound only by the supreme law of the nation. The court system is divided into four tiers: the Supreme Court, High Courts, District Courts, and Summary Courts.

The Supreme Court of Okaiken ({{nihongo|最高裁判所|Saikō saibansho}}) is the court of last resort with the power of judicial review. It consists of a Chief Justice, nominated by the Cabinet and appointed by the Emperor, and associate justices appointed by the Cabinet. A unique feature of the judicial system is the lay judge ({{nihongo|裁判員|saiban-in}}) system, introduced in 2009, which allows citizens to participate in serious criminal trials alongside professional judges. Additionally, the legal culture emphasizes wakai ({{nihongo|和解|settlement}}) by mutual agreement, which holds the same legal weight as a court judgment.

==Political Parties and Elections==
{{Further|List of political parties in Okaiken|Elections in Okaiken}}
Okaiken operates under a competitive multi-party system, which underwent a major realignment following the 60th general election on 27 October 2025. The political landscape is currently dominated by four major organizations, each representing distinct historical and ideological threads of Sanese society.

===Progressive Party of Okaiken===
{{Main|Progressive Party of Okaiken}}
The Progressive Party of Okaiken ({{nihongo|岡井県新法党|Okaiken Shinpōtō}}), or PPO, is a centre-left social democratic party and the senior partner in the current coalition government. Founded in 1952, the party has historically served as the primary alternative to conservative rule. Under the leadership of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]], the PPO advocates for a social market economy and a robust welfare state, notably passing the National Universal Health Act in 2023. Although the party lost 20 seats in the 2025 election, it remains the largest force in the House of Commoners with 181 seats. The PPO platform emphasizes social progressivism, environmental protection, and a pragmatic approach to international cooperation.

===Liberal Democratic Party===
{{Main|Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)}}
The Liberal Democratic Party ({{nihongo|自由民主党|Jiyū-Minshutō}}), known as the LDP or Jimintō, is the principal conservative force in Okaiken and the current Leader of the Opposition. Established in 1977, the party dominated the late 20th century under the 1977 System, presiding over periods of significant economic growth and the Jutsu Restoration. Ideologically, the LDP is a big-tent conservative organization, ranging from moderate fiscal liberals to nationalist factions that favor constitutional revision and a stronger military. Under President [[Koichiro Sadanum]]a, the party experienced a resurgence in 2025, gaining 40 seats to finish with 128, campaigning on a platform of fiscal competence and social stability.

===Okaiken Socialist Party===
{{Main|Okaiken Socialist Party}}
The Okaiken Socialist Party ({{nihongo|岡井県社会党|Okaiken Shakaitō}}), or OSP, is a left-wing democratic socialist party and the junior partner in the Honda coalition. The party has a volatile history, including a ban from 1968 to 1988 following an authoritarian attempt to abolish parliament under Genzui Kusaka. After decades as a minor player, the OSP saw a historic resurgence in 2025 under the populist leadership of [[Rento Nakayama]]. The Nakayama-mania phenomenon propelled the party from 2 seats to 50, fueled by a social-media-driven campaign focusing on anti-austerity, youth engagement, and non-interventionism.

===Sanese Communist Party===
{{Main|Sanese Communist Party}}
The Sanese Communist Party ({{nihongo|日本共産党|Okaiken Kyōsantō}}), or SCP, is a long-standing left-wing organization that governed Okaiken from 2016 to 2021 under Sho Aoyagi. The party advocates for a democratic revolution to achieve a socialist society and is a staunch defender of the pacifist Article 9. However, the party suffered a catastrophic collapse in the 2025 election, falling from 90 seats to 45. This defeat was largely attributed to high inflation during their previous tenure and a public relations disaster involving former leader [[Shintaro Hamabe]], who was filmed disparaging a small business owner. The party currently sits in the opposition, undergoing a period of internal restructuring.

